n't before  as the tool continues to be adopted into  various workflows  over the recent years we are starting to  see another paradigm shift with an  increase in adoption of crispr cas9  technology for therapeutics development  given the recent clinical successes and  commercial approvals in this space we  have also observed an exponential growth  in clinical trial pipelines as well  shown on the graph on the left hand side  you'll see that over the past few years  we have seen an exponential increase in  trials in said pipeline  we have also seen some cell and gene  therapies approved by the fda shown on  the right hand side  does further demonstrate the promise and  growth in the application of crispr  cast-9 technology for therapeutic  development  when thinking about key steps within the  translational pipeline there are four  distinct stages as researchers move  their project from bench to the clinic  from early research and develop  development to pre-clinical studies to  clinical trials and finally  commercialization  as researchers move that project through  this pipeline the requirements for the  critical reagents needed for that  project change  researchers start to look for higher  quality grade reagents to meet increased  quality and regulatory requirements  and often increased volume of reagents  as the demand for that project changed  from small scale to a large  manufacturing scale  now let's talk in more details about the  three key requirements that therapeutic  researchers look for when selecting  their critical reagents to support the  cell and  gene therapy  programs  firstly quality  researchers need to know that the  reagents they select can stand up to  increase quality and regulatory  requirements  secondly supply  researchers are looking for reassurance  that they will be able to purchase the  right amount of reagents they need at  the right time to support their programs  so it is incredibly important for these  researchers that the supplier of their  critical reagents will be able to  produce large lot size consistently  lastly and just as important performance  researchers need and want the highest  performing reagents possible  this means that not only must the  reagents be high performing but it must  also have minimal lot to lot variation  so that they can trust that lot number  one will perform just as well as lot  number 100  these are the three important factors  that researchers look for in their  reagents for their therapeutics programs  and factors to keep in mind when  developing gmp manufactured cath9  protein  with that we have recently launched the  gibco cts truecut cast 9 protein a next  generation cas9 protein designed and  manufactured to support clinical  research programs  our protein maintain high editing  efficiency in primary immune cells and  is manufactured in compliance with gmp  principles  it is also manufactured to meet the  requirements for the standards for  ancillary materials for cell gene and  tissue based products  to ensure adherence to the quality and  regulatory standards we leverage an  aseptic manufacturing process and our  protein is also subjected to extensive  safety testing including sterility  endotoxin and residual hose nucleic acid  and protein  as the latest addition to thermo fischer  scientific cell therapy systems family  of product cts true cut cas9 protein is  designed for cell therapy manufacturing  our cts product is manufactured to  conform with 21 cfr part 820 where  applicable and all at iso 13485  certified site cts products are also  subjected to extensive safety testing  and have traceability documentation  including fda's drug master files  finally our cts product brands have  proven use in several approved cell  therapies and and over 100 clinical  trials  an aspect that is also critical for the  development of gmp manufactured cas9 is  in having the appropriate manufacturing  facilities  it is important that the protein is made  in facilities that can meet the  appropriate quality and regulatory  requirements  as for our cts true cut kas9 product it  is made at our biosciences center of  excellence with dedicated gmp suites and  clean room facilities in accordance to  several regulatory directives and  guidelines  in looking at the manufacturing process  design and controls needed for the  development of gmp manufactured cast 9  shown here is an example schematic of  the manufacturing process  to further control the risk of  contaminants a closed system  manufacturing process that leverage  single-use technology should be used  additional controls from a facility  management perspective is also critical  for example all materials that enter gmp  facilities should be animal origin free  to the secondary level and  beta-lactam-free  to further reduce any risk of  contaminants  a few other considerations for the  manufacturing process of gmp cast 9  include  a process that is capable of producing  large lot size is important  as alluded to earlier having access to a  large single lot can allow researchers  to use the same material lot to support  that program  this could further minimize any  potential variability that may be  introduced when using materials from  different lots  our manufacturing process has a high  yield and can make upwards of 15 gram of  cas9 protein per purification run  to further control contaminant risk  several thorough filtration steps are  designed and added to the process  additionally  in-process controls are also  planned  and designed throughout the  manufacturing workflow as well  furthermore sterile fillings should be  completed in the appropriate cleanroom  suites and safety precautions taken  taken to further reduce the risk of  contaminants  for our manufacturing process all  containers are sterilized with  additional quality control in place  including performing a bioburden test on  the glove used after the assembling step  finally the final filled products are 